| ~4ct | [GIA E-IF](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28954758?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$106,765](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28954758?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [IGI D-FL 4ct](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28794225?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | [$15,908](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28794225?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) | $90,857 saved | At 4ct, lab saves $90K and gives a better grade |

**Verdict:** Natural wins on one thing only — mined origin and the secondary market that comes with it. On every other measurable: size, clarity, color, and price, lab dominates at 2ct and above. The $16,000–$21,000 figure in the title is the 2ct gap. At 3ct it's $28,000. At 4ct it's $90,000.

