## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is a lab grown asscher cut diamond?

A lab grown asscher cut diamond is a real diamond — same carbon crystal structure, same hardness, same optical properties as a mined asscher — grown in a laboratory using HPHT or CVD process instead of forming underground over billions of years. It is not a simulant or imitation. A gemologist cannot distinguish it from natural without specialized equipment.

### Is a lab grown asscher cut diamond a real diamond?

Yes. It is chemically, physically, and optically identical to a mined diamond. The FTC (Federal Trade Commission) requires retailers to disclose lab origin, but confirmed that lab grown diamonds are diamonds. The word "synthetic" technically applies but is misleading — it implies imitation, which lab diamonds are not.

### What is the difference between a lab created and synthetic asscher cut diamond?

"Lab created" and "lab grown" are the correct industry terms. "Synthetic" is an older term that is technically accurate but commonly misunderstood as meaning fake. All three terms refer to the same thing: a real diamond grown in a controlled environment. Simulants like moissanite and cubic zirconia are not diamonds and are not the same as lab grown diamonds.

### What does IGI certified asscher cut diamond mean?

IGI (International Gemological Institute) is the world's largest grader of lab-grown diamonds. An IGI certified lab grown asscher cut diamond has been independently assessed for cut, color, clarity, and carat weight. The certificate includes proportion data — depth, table, and symmetry — which is especially important for step-cuts like asscher where those numbers directly affect the hall-of-mirrors effect.

### How much does a lab grown asscher cut diamond cost in {{year}}?

Certified market data shows IGI lab grown asscher cut diamonds start at [$2,795 for 1.52ct D-IF](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). At 2ct, D-IF IGI is [$5,061 for 2.29ct](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991135?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). At 3ct, D-IF starts at [$8,849](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/26682894?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). At 4ct, D-IF is [$15,359](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28329443?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

### Lab grown vs natural asscher cut diamond — which should I buy?

Lab if you want maximum size and grade for your budget. Natural if mined origin, rarity value, or traditional secondary market resale matters to you. At 2ct, lab saves $16,000–$21,000 over natural at equivalent or better specs. At 3ct the savings exceed $28,000. At 4ct, lab saves $90,000. The [loose asscher cut diamond guide](/loose-asscher-cut-diamond) covers the full natural vs lab comparison.

### Should I buy IGI or GIA certified lab grown asscher cut diamond?

IGI for personal use. IGI is the industry-leading lab for lab-grown diamond certification, calibrated specifically for lab stones, accepted by all insurers and retailers. GIA lab certificates cost 10–20% more and buy you a smaller stone at the same budget. The only case for GIA lab is if you plan to sell privately to buyers who specifically require GIA.

### What clarity should I get for a lab grown asscher cut diamond?

D-IF is the correct target in lab. VS1 is the absolute minimum. VS2 is a mistake on any asscher cut because the step-cut facets expose inclusions that brilliant cuts hide. In lab, D-IF costs $2,795 for 1.52ct — there is no financial justification for buying VS2 or VS1 when IF is within reach.

### What color grade is best for a lab grown asscher cut diamond?

D. In lab, D color costs only $200–$400 more than F or G at 1.5ct, and $400–$600 more at 2ct. Asscher's step-cut facets show body color more than brilliant cuts — G color shows warmth in certain lighting. Pay the premium for D. It is small enough in lab that there is no reason to compromise.

### Does a lab grown asscher cut diamond look different from natural?

No. Face-up in a ring, under any lighting, a lab grown asscher cut diamond and a natural asscher are visually identical. Both produce the same hall-of-mirrors step-cut effect. A gemologist with a loupe cannot tell them apart. Only specialized laboratory equipment that detects trace growth-process elements can make the distinction.

### What is CVD vs HPHT lab grown asscher cut diamond?

CVD (Chemical Vapor Deposition) and HPHT (High Pressure High Temperature) are the two methods used to grow lab diamonds. CVD grows diamond layers from a carbon-rich gas. HPHT mimics the conditions deep in the earth. For buyers, the difference is minor — both produce real diamonds graded by IGI or GIA. Neither method is visually distinguishable. The certificate will usually note the growth method.

### Is a lab grown asscher cut diamond good for an engagement ring?

Yes — it is excellent. Asscher was the original Art Deco engagement ring cut, and lab grown stones give you the size to do the look justice at a price that leaves budget for a quality Art Deco setting. A [2ct+ lab asscher in a white gold or platinum setting](/asscher-cut-diamond-engagement-rings) is one of the most striking engagement rings you can buy.

### How do I verify an IGI certificate for a lab grown asscher cut diamond?

Every IGI certificate has a unique report number. Verify it on the IGI website (igi.org) before purchase. Confirm the report number on the stone matches the certificate. Check that depth percentage falls in the 60–68% range and table percentage in the 60–68% range. Confirm the stone is listed as "Lab-Grown" and the growth method (CVD or HPHT) is noted.

### What size lab grown asscher cut diamond gives the best value?

The [1.98ct D-FL at $3,848](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29230615?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) and the [2.29ct D-IF at $5,061](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991135?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) are the two best-value picks. The 1.98ct avoids the 2ct price threshold and saves $1,662 vs the 2ct equivalent. The 2.29ct is the sweet spot of size, grade, and price — 2ct+ in D-IF for just over $5K.

